# Hardware Product Design (Open-Source Toolchain)

Design reliable, production-ready electronic hardware as **code and scripted models**. From formal requirements and circuit simulation to KiCad schematic/layout, parametric 3D enclosure modeling, thermal/EMC validation, firmware scaffolding, and complete fabrication releases (Gerbers, drill, pick-and-place, and standard-formatted BOMs).

---

## When to Use This Skill
- Designing a new electronic product or embedded hardware device from concept to fabrication files.
- Creating or modifying schematics, PCB layouts, 3D mechanical enclosures, BOMs, or circuit simulations.
- Sizing and tuning passive components (filters, feedback loops, power converters) against a numerical specification.
- Generating manufacturing packages for PCB assembly (JLCPCB, PCBWay, Oshpark) and 3D printing/CNC enclosure fabrication.
- Preparing hardware bring-up plans, test point verification procedures, or EMC/ESD pre-compliance reviews.

## Do Not Use This Skill When
- The task is purely high-level software with zero physical circuit or hardware interaction.
- The project mandates proprietary enterprise EDA tools (Altium Designer, SolidWorks, Autodesk Fusion 360, Ansys) unless the user requests migration to or interoperation with open-source formats.

---

## The Open-Source Hardware Toolchain

Always prioritize headless CLI tools and scriptable Python APIs to guarantee end-to-end reproducibility:

| Stage | Primary Tools | Automation / CLI Command |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Schematic & PCB** | KiCad 8.x / 9.x | `kicad-cli sch erc`, `kicad-cli pcb drc`, `pcbnew` Python API |
| **Output Automation** | KiBot, KiKit | `kikit panelize`, automated Gerber/BOM generation |
| **3D CAD & Enclosure** | `build123d` (preferred), CadQuery, OpenSCAD | `python -m build123d`, `openscad -o enclosure.stl`, `freecadcmd` |
| **Circuit Simulation** | ngspice, Qucs-S, PySpice | `ngspice -b -r output.raw circuit.cir` |
| **Parameter Tuning** | `scipy.optimize`, `pymoo` | Python Monte Carlo sweeps & E-series snapping (`scripts/`) |
| **Thermal & Structural** | Elmer FEM, CalculiX (via FreeCAD FEM) | Scripted meshes via Gmsh, batch solver execution |
| **Signal Integrity / RF** | openEMS, Elmer | FDTD electromagnetic simulation, S-parameter extraction |
| **Firmware Scaffolding** | PlatformIO, Zephyr RTOS, ESP-IDF | `pio run`, `west build`, `arduino-cli` |
| **Virtual Bring-up** | Renode, QEMU | Headless MCU emulation and peripheral register verification |
| **Manufacturing Slicing** | OrcaSlicer, PrusaSlicer, CuraEngine | `prusa-slicer --export-gcode enclosure.stl` |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Pre-Flight Environment Check**: Before beginning, audit installed tools using:
> ```bash
> where.exe kicad-cli ngspice freecadcmd openscad pio python
> ```
> Explicitly report missing tools to the user rather than failing silently or substituting unapproved proprietary alternatives.

---

## Standard Project Directory Architecture

Maintain this disciplined workspace structure for every hardware repository:

```
project-root/
  docs/
    requirements.md         # Specs, operating envelopes, environmental ratings, certifications
    architecture.md         # System block diagrams, power trees (Mermaid), IC selections
    bringup.md              # Staged first-article power-up checklist & verification measurements
  hardware/
    <project>.kicad_pro     # KiCad project file
    <project>.kicad_sch     # Schematic hierarchy
    <project>.kicad_pcb     # Board layout and design rules
    libraries/              # Project-specific custom footprints, symbols, and 3D models
  mech/
    model.py                # Parametric build123d / CadQuery enclosure script
    pcb.step                # Exported board geometry from KiCad
    enclosure.step          # Production 3D solid model
    enclosure.stl           # 3D printing mesh
    drawings/               # 2D dimensional DXF drawings
  sim/
    circuits/               # ngspice .cir netlists, subcircuits, and testbenches
    tune.py                 # Parameter sweep & optimization driver
    results/                # Generated Bode plots, transient plots, and Monte Carlo curves
  firmware/
    platformio.ini          # Build environment configuration
    src/
      main.c / main.cpp
      pins.h                # Hardware pinout contract mapped directly to schematic netlist
  fab/
    gerbers/                # RS-274X / Gerber X2 copper, mask, silk, and drill files
    bom.csv / bom.xlsx      # SMT assembly bill of materials (LCSC/DigiKey part numbers)
    cpl.csv                 # Component placement centroid list (pick-and-place)
  scripts/                  # Project automation utilities (ERC/DRC runners, export hooks)
```

---

## The 10-Stage Product Design Workflow

Follow this sequence strictly. Commit artifacts to Git after completing each stage.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    S1["1. Requirements"] --> S2["2. Architecture & Power Tree"]
    S2 --> S3["3. Schematic & ERC"]
    S3 --> S4["4. Simulation & Parameter Tuning"]
    S4 --> S5["5. PCB Layout & DRC"]
    S5 --> S6["6. 3D Enclosure & Clearances"]
    S6 --> S7["7. Thermal & Mechanical Check"]
    S7 --> S8["8. Firmware & Pinout Contract"]
    S8 --> S9["9. Fabrication Release (fab/)"]
    S9 --> S10["10. Bring-up & Verification Plan"]
```

### 1. Requirements Formulation (`docs/requirements.md`)
Define quantifiable metrics before selecting components:
- **Functional Requirements**: Inputs, outputs, human interface (buttons, LEDs, OLED), communication buses (USB, BLE, Wi-Fi, CAN, I2C, SPI).
- **Power Budget & Envelope**: Supply sources (USB 5V, Li-ion 3.7V, 12V/24V DC, battery chemistry), maximum sleep current ($< 20\,\mu\text{A}$), peak burst current, battery run life.
- **Environmental & Mechanical**: Enclosure size envelope ($L \times W \times H$ mm), weight limit, operating temperature range (e.g. $-20^\circ\text{C}$ to $+70^\circ\text{C}$), IP water/dust resistance rating, drop tolerance.
- **Cost & Target Fab**: Target BOM cost at target volume (e.g. $<\$15$ @ 1k units), target assembly provider (JLCPCB, PCBWay).
- **Compliance Scope**: FCC Part 15 Class B, CE RED, RoHS/WEEE.

### 2. Architecture & Power Tree (`docs/architecture.md`)
- Author a Mermaid system diagram and power tree showing voltages, maximum currents, and conversion efficiencies:
  ```mermaid
  flowchart LR
    VIN["VBUS (5V, 2A)"] --> PROT["TVS + Reverse Polarity PFET"]
    PROT --> BUCK["Buck Converter 3.3V (92% Eff)"]
    PROT --> CHG["Li-Ion Linear Charger (4.2V)"]
    BUCK --> MCU["MCU Core (3.3V, 80mA)"]
    BUCK --> SENS["Sensors & Radio (3.3V, 120mA)"]
  ```
- **Component Selection Strategy**:
  - Prioritize components listed as "Basic" or "Preferred" in assembly catalogs (e.g. JLCPCB basic parts library) to eliminate reel-loading surcharges.
  - Check component lifecycle: Reject components marked NRND (Not Recommended for New Designs) or Obsolete.
  - Ensure every IC has readily accessible KiCad symbols and footprints.

### 3. Schematic Capture & Electrical Rule Checks
Draft the schematic hierarchically in KiCad. Enforce standard defensive design:
- **Power Protection**: TVS diode array at external power input; P-channel MOSFET or ideal diode controller for reverse polarity protection; PTC resettable fuse for overcurrent.
- **ESD Suppression**: Low-capacitance ESD arrays ($C_j < 1\,\text{pF}$) on high-speed USB/data lines located immediately adjacent to connectors.
- **Decoupling Hierarchy**: Every IC power pin must have local ceramic decoupling ($0.1\,\mu\text{F} / 100\,\text{nF}$) placed physically closest to the pin, backed by bulk capacitance ($4.7\,\mu\text{F} - 22\,\mu\text{F}$) on the rail.
- **Debug & Programming**: Standardized debug headers (SWD 10-pin 1.27mm, Tag-Connect, or labeled 2.54mm test pads) including `SWDIO`, `SWCLK`, `NRST`, `GND`, and `VCC`.
- **Test Points**: Include accessible 1mm surface-mount test pads on every power rail, reset line, and critical communication bus.
- **ERC Verification Gate**:
  ```bash
  kicad-cli sch erc --severity-all --exit-code-violations hardware/<project>.kicad_sch
  ```
  *Requirement: Zero errors and zero unacknowledged warnings.*

### 4. Circuit Simulation & Parameter Tuning (`sim/`)
Simulate critical subcircuits in ngspice before placing PCB traces:
- **Power Stages**: Buck/boost switching ripple, inductor saturation margin, and transient load step response.
- **Analog Signal Chains**: Filter cutoff, gain bandwidth, phase margin, and input impedance.
- **Simulation Execution**:
  ```bash
  ngspice -b -r sim/results/transient.raw sim/circuits/power_filter.cir
  ```
- **Automated Parameter Optimization**:
  - Parameterize netlists and use Python drivers (`scipy.optimize.minimize` or `pymoo` for multi-objective problems).
  - Snap optimized floating-point values to nearest **E24** (5%) or **E96** (1%) standard values using the bundled snapper script:
    ```bash
    python scripts/e_series_snapper.py --value 4732 --series E96  # Output: 4750 ohms
    ```
  - Re-run Monte Carlo simulations (500 runs) across component tolerance bounds ($\pm 1\%$ resistors, $\pm 10\%$ capacitors) and temperature coefficients.

### 5. PCB Layout, Stackup & Design Rules (`hardware/`)
- **Layer Stackup**:
  - Prefer 4-layer boards for any design containing high-speed signals ($>10\,\text{MHz}$), switching regulators, or wireless:
    - Layer 1 (Top): High-speed signals, component placement
    - Layer 2 (Inner 1): Solid uninterrupted Ground plane (GND)
    - Layer 3 (Inner 2): Power planes / signal routing
    - Layer 4 (Bottom): Secondary signals, ground fill
- **Design Rules Setup**: Set clearance, trace width, via size, and annular ring limits to match the specific fab's standard (e.g. min 5 mil trace/space, 0.3mm drill / 0.45mm via pad for JLCPCB).
- **Placement & Routing Strategy**:
  1. Fix mechanical constraints first: mounting holes, connectors, LEDs, switches matching the enclosure.
  2. Place decoupling capacitors directly at IC power pins with short, low-inductance via connections to GND plane.
  3. Keep switching power loops (inductor, diode/switch FET, input cap) compact and isolated from analog traces.
  4. Match differential pairs ($90\,\Omega$ USB, $100\,\Omega$ Ethernet) and ensure continuous ground return paths underneath.
- **DRC Verification Gate**:
  ```bash
  kicad-cli pcb drc --severity-all --exit-code-violations hardware/<project>.kicad_pcb
  ```
  *Requirement: Zero violations.*

### 6. 3D Enclosure Modeling & Interference Checks (`mech/`)
Export the board geometry as STEP and build the enclosure parametrically around it:
- **STEP Export**:
  ```bash
  kicad-cli pcb export step --subst-models -o mech/pcb.step hardware/<project>.kicad_pcb
  ```
- **Parametric Enclosure (`build123d` / `CadQuery`)**:
  - Read `mech/pcb.step` into the Python model.
  - Mount bosses: Outer diameter $D_{boss} \approx 2.0 \times D_{screw}$, pilot hole diameter $D_{pilot} \approx 0.8 \times D_{screw}$ for self-tapping thermoplastic screws (e.g. M2 or M2.5).
  - Process Tolerances:
    - FDM 3D Printing: $+0.35\,\text{mm}$ to $+0.40\,\text{mm}$ diametral clearance on mating parts.
    - SLA / Resin: $+0.15\,\text{mm}$ clearance.
    - CNC Milled Aluminum: $+0.10\,\text{mm}$ clearance.
  - Programmatic Collision Detection: Compute Boolean intersection between the enclosure solid and the PCB solid. If volume $> 0\,\text{mm}^3$, output coordinates of overlapping components.

### 7. Thermal & Structural Verification
- **Thermal Dissipation Check**:
  - Calculate total power loss for linear regulators, switching FETs, and MCUs:
    $$T_j = T_a + P_{loss} \cdot \left(\theta_{jc} + \theta_{ca}\right)$$
  - Ensure estimated junction temperature $T_j$ stays below $85^\circ\text{C}$ ($105^\circ\text{C}$ absolute maximum).
  - Add thermal via arrays (0.3mm drill, 1.0mm pitch) under exposed thermal ground pads.
- **Stress & Drop Evaluation**:
  - Run Elmer FEM or FreeCAD FEM CalculiX simulation for snap-fit latches, button flexures, or drop impact when specified in requirements.

### 8. Firmware Scaffolding & Pinout Contract (`firmware/`)
- **Authoritative Pin Contract**:
  - Generate or maintain `firmware/include/pins.h` synced directly to the KiCad netlist.
  - Map every GPIO to its hardware function, internal pull-up/down requirements, and interrupt capabilities.
- **PlatformIO Scaffold**:
  - Create `platformio.ini` with target board, framework (Arduino, Zephyr, ESP-IDF), build flags, and monitor baud rate.
  - Implement baseline hardware self-test: power rail ADC reads, I2C bus scanner, and blinking status LED.
- **State Machine Definition**:
  - Document operational states (BOOT, IDLE, ACTIVE, LOW_POWER_SLEEP, FAULT) using Mermaid state diagrams.

### 9. Fabrication Package Release (`fab/`)
Generate clean, verified manufacturing files in `fab/`:
- **Gerber & Drill Generation**:
  ```bash
  kicad-cli pcb export gerbers -o fab/gerbers/ hardware/<project>.kicad_pcb
  kicad-cli pcb export drill -o fab/gerbers/ hardware/<project>.kicad_pcb
  ```
- **Component Placement File (CPL / Centroid)**:
  ```bash
  kicad-cli pcb export pos --format csv --units mm -o fab/cpl.csv hardware/<project>.kicad_pcb
  ```
- **Bill of Materials (BOM)**:
  - Generate formatted CSV / Excel BOM compatible with target fab (Columns: `Designator`, `Val`, `Package`, `LCSC Part #`, `Manufacturer`, `MPN`, `Quantity`).
  - Verify every component status is active and in-stock.
- **Enclosure Fabrication Files**:
  - Export production STEP: `enclosure_top.step`, `enclosure_bottom.step`.
  - Export 3D printing STL: `enclosure_top.stl`, `enclosure_bottom.stl` (high chordal tolerance $\le 0.05\,\text{mm}$).

### 10. Prototype Bring-Up Plan (`docs/bringup.md`)
Deliver a step-by-step verification checklist for initial board bring-up:
1. **Visual Inspection**: Check for solder bridges, tombstoned passives, IC orientation pin-1 markers under a microscope.
2. **Cold Resistance Checks (Unpowered)**:
   - Measure resistance from each power rail (`VBUS`, `3V3`, `VBAT`) to `GND`.
   - Reject power-up if resistance is below expected impedance (short circuit detection).
3. **Current-Limited Power-Up**:
   - Power up using a bench supply limited to $50\,\text{mA}$.
   - Verify supply current does not rail against the current limit.
4. **Voltage Rail Verification**:
   - Probe each test point with a DMM: check nominal voltages ($3.3\text{V} \pm 2\%$).
   - Check switching regulator ripple with an oscilloscope.
5. **Debug Probe & Firmware Bring-up**:
   - Connect SWD / JTAG debug probe; read MCU Device ID.
   - Flash minimal test firmware to verify clocks, UART console, and I2C/SPI peripheral connectivity.

---

## Parameter Tuning & Optimization Guidelines

1. **Numerical Objective Formulation**:
   - Formulate tuning problems mathematically (e.g. minimize ripple $V_{rip}(L, C)$ subject to $f_{sw} \ge 500\,\text{kHz}$, $I_{L,peak} < I_{sat}$, and cost $\le \$0.80$).
2. **Algorithm Selection**:
   - Single objective (e.g. RC filter cutoff): `scipy.optimize.minimize_scalar` or Brent's method.
   - Constrained multi-parameter: `scipy.optimize.minimize` (SLSQP or Nelder-Mead).
   - Multi-objective trade-offs (e.g. efficiency vs transient recovery time vs board area): `pymoo` with NSGA-II to extract the Pareto frontier.
3. **E-Series Value Snapping**:
   - Never leave theoretical values (e.g. $4.712\,\text{k}\Omega$) in the schematic. Snap to standard E24/E96 values and re-simulate to confirm performance margins.
4. **Tolerance Worst-Case & Yield**:
   - Run 500+ Monte Carlo iterations varying parameters by their component tolerances ($\pm 1\%$, $\pm 5\%$, $\pm 10\%$, $\pm 20\%$) and temperature drifts. Report 3-sigma worst-case metrics.

---

## Non-Negotiable Engineering Rules

1. **Proof of Verification**: Never declare a schematic, PCB, or simulation complete without running `kicad-cli sch erc`, `kicad-cli pcb drc`, or ngspice and presenting the exact CLI output.
2. **No Fictional Components**: Never invent component MPNs, footprints, pin assignments, or electrical ratings. If a part number is unconfirmed, flag it and prompt the user.
3. **Safety Critical Isolation**: Flag anything involving AC mains ($>48\,\text{V}$), lithium-ion battery charging without protection ICs, high currents ($>5\,\text{A}$), or RF transmitters requiring FCC certification. Mandate qualified engineering review.
4. **Script-First Principle**: Every build step, export, and simulation must be executable headlessly via CLI or Python script. If a GUI action is unavoidable, supply explicit step-by-step instructions.
5. **Disciplined Interaction**: Ask at most one concise clarifying question at a time, and only when the choice fundamentally alters hardware architecture.
6. **Progress Transparency**: Conclude each design turn with a concise 3-part ledger:
   - What was accomplished and verified.
   - Files created or modified.
   - The immediate next task.
